## Reader Standard

**Pass.** The prose contains no profanity, obscenity, crude slang, blasphemy, sexual content, or innuendo. The floor warden's oath is correctly rendered only as “a word Cael had never once heard from staff.” Violence has fear, bodily cost, recovery time, and institutional consequence without gore. Courage, honesty, loyalty, restraint, consent, care, and accountability are costly choices; the text does not reward cruelty or pretend the proposed acquisition is clean.

The principal thirteen-year-old-reader risk is conceptual density, not vocabulary. The moral debate remains comprehensible because Cael ultimately states three numbered rules and then tests his own reasoning for bias. Preserve that plain procedural spine during any polish.

