# Chapter 21 — Instruments

There was a first-year on the third practice ring who would not stop.

It had been raining since the morning, a thin grey rain that made the chalk run and the boards slick, and the tiers were nearly empty. Cael was there because he was always there. The first-year was there because, as far as Cael could tell, she had decided the season was going to be won by whoever turned up on the days nobody else did. She was a Current practitioner, small and wiry, with her hair plastered flat to her skull, and she had fought four practice bouts that afternoon and lost three of them. Between bouts she stood under the eaves of the north stair, wringing water out of her sleeves, watching the next pair with a ferocious attention, and then she put her name on the slate again.

Her fifth was against a Blade second-year a head taller than she was, and Cael watched it from the empty fourth tier with the rain running off the brim of his hood.

She lost the first touch in about the time it took to blink. The Blade boy came in long and straight and she rode his line the way Current fighters were taught, going along the force instead of against it, and the boards were wet, and her back foot went out from under her on the chalk. She sat down hard. The instructor at the table said "Touch, Blade," without lifting his eyes from the register. She got up. She did not look at the table, or at the tiers, or at anything but the Blade boy's lead foot.

The second touch was hers. It was not pretty. She went in low, deliberately short, on the wet board where her own foot had failed her, and when he came long and straight again she did not ride his line at all. She let it go over her shoulder and came up inside it with her palm on his breastbone, so close he could not have struck her if he had tried. The boy blinked down at the hand on his chest as if somebody had put it there while he was not looking.

He took the third and the fourth, cleanly, because he was a year older and a head taller and simply better, and the bout was his three to one. She shook his hand. Then she went back under the eaves of the north stair and wrung out her sleeves again, and stood with her eyes on the next pair, and Cael saw her mouth moving as if she were saying something over to herself so as not to lose it.

He did not know her name. He wrote her down by her smock and her Path and the touch she had taken off the wet board, *went short where she slipped; used the slip*, and then, because he was curious, went to look for her on the sheet.

She was seventy-fourth.

He did the sum on the steps, roughly, in his head, and then did not believe it. So he went down to the long reading room under the law range and did it properly, on paper, with the session calendar from the board by the theater door and a copy of Bracken's half-sheet on seeding laid beside it.

The ladder's rules he already knew by heart. A challenge went up the column, four places at the most, and a win took the loser's place. So the quickest any name could climb was four places in an afternoon, every afternoon, against the highest name it was allowed to reach, without a loss.

The calendar was the part nobody had put beside the rules.

It gave a Copper name a bout about once in six days, when the pairings fell kindly. From the posting to the final was a little over a hundred days. Seventeen bouts at the very most, then, and the first of the seventeen was the drawn bout, which counted in the record and moved nobody on the sheet. Sixteen that could move a name. And that was for a name that was never injured, never deferred, never left standing on its afternoon without anybody to fight.

He put the first-year's number in. Seventy-fourth. To reach the four names that would fight the final she needed to climb seventy places, four at a time, which was eighteen wins in a row.

She had sixteen bouts.

He sat and looked at it. He went up the column a line at a time to find where the arithmetic opened again, and found it at sixty-eighth. From sixty-eighth, a perfect season would just reach the final. From sixty-ninth and every line below it, no season could. Twenty-three names at the foot of the Copper sheet, a quarter of it, could not reach the crown even if they never lost a bout from the posting to the last day. They had been unable to reach it since the porter pressed the first pin into the board. Nothing had shut them out but a calendar and a rule, each of which, taken alone, was perfectly sensible.

And every one of the twenty-three, so far as he could tell, was out on the rings in the rain, fighting for it.

He did the sum again from the other end, slowly, then took it two floors up to carrel eleven and laid it on the commentary Karis was reading.

She read it with her finger under each line. She checked the calendar against the copy in her own notes, and the half-sheet against her memory of the half-sheet, which was better than the half-sheet. Then she turned the paper over and did the whole thing herself on the back, in her quick small hand, and came out where he had come out. She sat back and looked more annoyed than he had seen her all term, which from Karis was the highest praise a piece of work could get.

"Twenty-three," she said.

"Twenty-three. There's a girl in a Current smock fighting on the third ring in the rain right now who's seventy-fourth."

"It isn't malicious."

"I know it isn't."

"Somebody drew up that calendar years ago, for a shorter column. Somebody else wrote the four-place rule for another reason entirely, and probably a good one. Nobody's ever laid one on top of the other." She tapped the foot of the sheet, where the twenty-three were. "The people it shuts out are all down here. Nobody down here has been taught how to do this sum. And nobody up at the top has ever needed to."

"Somehow that's worse."

"It almost always is," said Karis, already writing. "Can I keep this?"

"It's yours. I made a copy."

"Of course you did." She did not look up. "Is anybody going to tell her? The girl on the third ring?"

Cael thought about it for a long time.

"I don't know," he said. "If somebody told me there was no way through, I'd want to know. And then I'd go out and fight anyway, to see." He looked at the sum. "I think she already would. I think that's why she's out there."

---

There was another way to read the same sheet, from the top down, and that was the way the rest of the bluff read it.

Cael took it to Bracken, because it was a matter of record, and Bracken was the record.

The registrar looked up from the same bare desk in the same bare room with the expression of a man who has been stopped halfway down a long column and would like to be told the interruption is worth the column. Cael asked him whether the season's standings decided the tournament squad.

"No," said Bracken. He laid his pen down square to the desk's edge. "They are evidence. They are admissible. They are not a verdict. I would be grateful if nobody in your hearing confused those three words. Nor a standing with a rank. A standing is this season's chalk. A rank is what the registry has written on a certificate, and the registry does not read our wall." He folded his hands. "Next year this academy will contest the qualifying season for the Continental. The chancellor has said so in an open address; it is on the record, so I may repeat it. A squad will be chosen. The people choosing it will read this season's closing standings among a great deal else. A strong season will not put a name on that squad. A weak one will very probably keep a name off it." He picked his pen back up. "Your friend from the north stands near the foot of a column she plainly does not belong in. If she climbs, it will be noticed. If she does not, that will be noticed too. That is all I may tell you, and rather more than I ought."

It explained the upper ends of the boards, which had puzzled Cael for a week. The Iron column and the thin Silver sheet fought every session with a kind of grim care, as if each afternoon were being written into a book that would be read aloud next year. That was exactly what each afternoon was. The names near their tops were not fighting for a circlet in a glass case. They were fighting to be read.

And it threw a hard new light on Fiske.

He watched her that week on a practice afternoon, fighting a nervous first-year as a courtesy, which the Copper holder did once a session by custom. She was not fighting him to win; she had won before the first exchange. She was fighting him to show him something. She let him come, and come again, and on the third time put her small flat push into the boards an inch in front of his lead foot, very slowly, so that he could feel exactly where it had gone and why he had stumbled. Then she told him in a few quiet words what she had done, and walked him back to his chalk, and did it again a little faster.

She was the best Copper fighter Cael had ever seen. Her certificate said Copper Rank Eight, and had said Rank Eight for two years. Rank, like tier, was a word an Arbiter wrote on a certificate after an advancement evaluation; no bout on any ladder could add a number to it, and while her paper said Copper, the Copper bracket was the only bracket she could fight in. No registry had written a word about Fiske in two years, because nobody had asked one to. Lira had worked out in a wash-house, over a pair of ruined shirts, why nobody had asked.

That left her the crown and nothing else. She had reached the top of the Copper sheet as a first-year, and had been standing up there ever since like a woman at the top of a stair with no landing. The only thing the ladder still had to offer her was a circlet she had already worn home twice.

Cael began her page that night as he began everybody's, with rhythm and weight and breath. By the end of it he found he had stopped writing about Fiske at all. He was writing about the cage.

---

He went to every session after that, all of them, not only the ones his friends fought in. Copper and Iron afternoons, the Silver sheet's rare bouts, the practice rings in between. Eleven Paths fought somewhere on the three sheets, and by the fourth session every one of them had its page. He did not expect all eleven to be right by the end of the season, and he wrote that on the first page too.

The Iron afternoons had Ephram, and Ephram had a rail.

He would not have called it a court. He came to the yard when the ladder told him to, fought, and won, without making anything of it; he was Iron Rank Six and the highest-ranked enrollee on the bluff, and he carried it like a man carrying an umbrella on a day it was not raining. But between his bouts he stood at the lower rail on the west side, and five or six of Rooke's cohort stood round him. When a bout on the floor ended he took it apart for them, exchange by exchange, in a low even voice he never raised, and the circle of people within hearing grew a little every afternoon, because people had learned to stop talking when he started.

On the second of those afternoons, at the outer edge of the circle, Cael saw a small wiry figure in a Current smock with her hair dried into a wild bush. It was the first-year from the third ring, seventy-fourth on the Copper sheet, standing on her toes behind two Blade fourth-years to hear what the best fighter on the bluff thought about a bout she would never be in. Nobody had told her she could not reach the final. Nobody had told her she was allowed to listen to Ephram, either. She had simply come, and was listening, with her lips moving. Cael found, to his surprise, that he was glad.

Cael listened when he could get near enough, which was seldom. Ephram never once turned his head. He had said his piece about Cael on the top tier in front of sixty people and was waiting now, as he had said he would, for the semester evaluation, and he waited the way he did everything, without fuss.

The commentary was good. Cael would have liked it to be worse. On the second Iron afternoon of the season a Current fighter lost on a counter she should have seen coming. Ephram explained to his circle precisely why: she had read the other fighter's shoulder, and the shoulder had been sold to her, and the hips had told the truth the whole time. It was exactly what Cael had written on his knee a minute before. It was not always right. A week later Ephram took a feint for a commitment because it had been thrown by a fighter he admired, and Cael wrote that down too, with some satisfaction. But two days after that he heard Ephram say to a second-year, quietly, *I had that wrong on Fourth-day; here's why*, and then explain why, and Cael wrote that down underlined.

The chancellor came to some of the sessions, and that was a page in itself.

She did not come to all of them, only the ones that mattered to the tops of the boards. And she never came alone. One afternoon two guild factors from Ostrand sat beside her on the faculty stand, in good dark coats with the river guild's badge at their collars, and said nothing for an hour. Another afternoon it was an old man whose name Cael had seen carved on the endowment board in the lecture stair. Twice it was a woman in the blue and grey of one of the large river-trade houses, who asked questions behind her glove during the bouts and nodded slowly at whatever Withrow answered.

Withrow hardly watched the floor on those afternoons. She watched the faces beside her watching it. It took him one afternoon to see what she was doing, and he found he could not hold it against her.

A school like this one lived by turning bruises into names on a wall, and names on a wall into a reputation, and reputation into money, and money into halls and brass grids and, next year, a qualifying berth on a continental stage. The Crown yard was the one place on the bluff where that whole long chain came out into the light where people could see it. Withrow had told him on his nineteenth day what she meant to have done with her chair before she gave it up. Here she was, doing it, in the plain open air, with a river-trade woman at her elbow.

And on every one of those afternoons, at every one of those sessions, somewhere high on the west side, there was a man with an armful of forms.

---

By the end of the month Cael did not need the map any more.

He would come through the north arch into the yard, or up the gallery stair, or out onto the second landing of the lecture range, and somewhere in the time it took him to count four, he would know. He did not look. The agreement said not to, and he had found he no longer had to. He asked himself the question he had carried onto every floor for three years, *where would I stand?*, and the bluff gave him one answer, and the answer always had a man in it. In the thick of a knot of people. High on the west. Never on yesterday's seat. Never at the bare end of a bench, where a body on its own is a thing the eye goes to. Never once alone.

Counting to four was not searching. It was working a sum he could now do the way he could tell a fighter's reach from the length of a forearm.

Twice, in the month's last week, the sum came out wrong.

Both were late sessions. Both times he came into the yard and went in his head to the best place, and the man was not on it. He was on the place beside it: the head of the north stair instead of the rail, most of the floor instead of all of it, with the stone of the stairwell close at his back.

Cael did not look. But that evening he went along the assessment wing's passage, where the wing posted its working hours on a board for anyone to read. He had every right to read it, since the same board set the hours of his own sessions. He found the two days. On each of them the wing had kept its counter open from the first bell to the last, with panels sitting all afternoon and a long run of practical assessments for the second-years behind them. Ten hours at a desk and on his feet, both days, for a man whose Path charged him a little for every hour he spent among people.

He took the two dates back to the fatigue page and set them in the margin beside the line that sloped down through the afternoon, and wrote beside them, without having to guess at anything, *long day; counter open first bell to last.* It was only two dots. He was more pleased with them than two dots deserved. The curve had said where the man would be before he was there, twice, and a judgment that could do that had begun, very slightly, to stand on a second leg.

He eased off the counting that week for four more days, at a time of his own choosing, and on the fourth evening Brom fed him at the post and the read came in on time, a dozen calls, clean. He wrote *two instances* in the Log. He did not let himself write *rule*.

Walking back along the coping afterward, Brom stopped to listen to a barge going down under the bluff, the long note and the two short, and the shorter answer from a landing somewhere below.

"I found out," he said. "Seventh-day, in Ostrand. There's a boy minds the lock gate by the fish steps for his uncle. I asked him."

"What does it say?"

"The long and the two short is *loaded, downstream, give way*." Brom looked enormously pleased with himself. "The two short on their own, coming back, means *heard you*. If you don't answer, the river guild fines you. If you answer wrong, they fine you twice." He listened to another one go by. "You've been writing that noise down for a month without knowing what it meant. I thought you'd want it."

Cael wrote it in the notebook with the date and Brom's name beside it, and left the boy's name out, because Brom had not asked it.

He heard the horns two hundred times a week from his window. Within a few days of knowing what they said, he found he had stopped hearing them at all.

---

The Log got its season entry late on Seventh-day. He wrote the inventory first, as he had opened every season he had ever logged, with the shutter back and the river noisy under the bluff.

*Five confirmed fragments.*

*Wind-adjacent. Ceiling on sprung timber: four free, fifth billed. On stone: three free. Landing beat the length it has always been. Never shortened.*

*Pressure-adjacent. Unused this term. Withheld at the baseline plate. On the record as not shown.*

*Iron-adjacent. A pace and a half. Blind to Lattice, and quiet about it; the rule in both books. A beat late while the counting runs. On time after four days off it, twice. Two instances. Not a rule.*

*Compression-adjacent. Unused. Untested on timber.*

*Ember-adjacent. No deployment; off the floor by agreement with Karis.*

A sixth line went under the five, alone. Standing still was a reading too, and readings got written down whether they were exciting or not.

*No change in any of the five since Greyvane.*

On the next page went the season's entry. It had been forming since the rain, the sum and the little brass stud, and it arrived in one piece with no crossings-out.

*Season's open. Lira's going to win the wrong crown. Brom's going to lose the right fights. And the Compact's man corrects my paperwork now. Everyone in this building is being measured by an instrument that doesn't fit them. I need to understand why the instruments are built this way. That's the semester's real question.*

He was still looking at it when somebody knocked, and Lira came in without waiting, with his spare pencil in her hand. She had borrowed it at breakfast. She put it on the desk, and her eyes went to the open page the way her eyes always went to a page with her name near it, and she read the entry upside down before he could think whether to close the book.

She did not say anything for a moment.

"The wrong crown," she said.

"Yes."

"It's the only one they've got." She turned the pencil a quarter turn on the desk with one finger, square to the edge. "I'll win it anyway. You know I will. I won't throw a final with eight hundred people watching just to make a point about paper."

"I know."

"And then they'll write *Copper* on my certificate the morning after, same as the morning before." She was quiet. "You've written down that it won't mean anything."

"I've written down that it won't mean anything about what you are." He looked at the page. "It'll mean a great deal about what they are."

Lira considered that for a long moment, the way she considered a feed she had decided to let go by.

"Brom's the other sentence," she said.

"Yes."

"He won't mind that. He'll like it, if you show him." She went to the door. "Don't show him tonight. His head still hurts." And then, with her hand on the latch, not looking round: "The last one's right. The question. I've been asking it since Fenmark, only I never had it in a sentence." She went out, and shut the door very quietly behind her.

Cael sat for a while. Then he got up and went to the window and leaned on the sill with the river going by in the dark below, its lamps and its barges and somewhere under the bluff a horn saying *heard you*.

Every door on the bluff had its card, in a little brass frame at the height of a man's eye: name, Path, tier, the ledger hung up where people walked. He went round some of them in his head, one by one.

*Wind. Copper.* On a door in the second quadrangle. Eleven seconds, eight hundred people, a Stone Path on his back.

*Iron Skin. Copper.* Four words in pencil on the outside of a wrap, by a man who had been hit too hard to trust his own memory as far as the gate, and had won anyway.

*Force. Copper.* On a fifth-year's door, with nothing on it anywhere to say she had held the crown two years, and nothing it could ever say about what she had become while she held it.

*Seln. Shadow. Bronze.* At the far end of the staff corridor. Bronze, because Bronze was easy to forget; and behind the card, work no certificate anywhere had a name for.

And his own, squeezed so small the letters barely fitted: *Cael. Assay provision. Evaluation by demonstration.* A boy of fifteen with five borrowed things and no Path of his own, who sat above a floor every afternoon that he was not allowed down onto, holding the one piece of paper in his life that told the plain truth about him.

Every one of them weighed wrong. Every one of them weighed carefully, by honest people doing honest work, on instruments that did not fit. And instruments did not grow out of the ground like grass on a bluff. They were made.

He did not write the next part. Questions went into the Log once he had earned them by work, and he had not earned this one yet. But he let himself think it, leaning on the sill, while the river talked to itself in the dark.

*Somebody built these instruments. Somebody chose what each one would look at and what it would look past. Nobody stumbles into a thing that exact. That was a decision.*

*I'd like to know who made it.*

