## 1. Source distance

### Method (the editorial review's, repeated)

Tokeniser of `tools/source_overlap.py`. Every manuscript sentence of ≥8 tokens matched to its best source sentence in ch15–17 by difflib ordered-token ratio, prefiltered by token-set Jaccard ≥ 0.15; ≥ 0.50 = kept skeleton, ≥ 0.35 = close paraphrase. Six-word maximal shared runs counted against the whole source book, per 10k words. Per chapter and per scene (split on `---`), pre-repair and post. Script: `align.py` in my scratch directory; figures below are mine and differ slightly from the coordinator's 6-word counts in scale (I count against the whole book; ranking and direction agree). Then every rebuilt scene read side by side with its source.

### Per chapter

| Ch | Skeleton ≥.50 pre → post | Close ≥.35 pre → post | 6-word runs /10k pre → post |
|---|---|---|---|
| 40 | 3% → 3% | 12% → 11% | 12 → 12 |
| 41 | 1% → 1% | 18% → 17% | 17 → 17 |
| 42 | 31% → **9%** | 56% → 37% | 131 → 49 |
| 43 | 34% → **10%** | 57% → 32% | 182 → 68 |
| 44 | 23% → **16%** | 47% → 35% | 179 → 85 |
| 45 | 18% → **9%** | 40% → 30% | 104 → 50 |
| 46 | 13% → **5%** | 31% → 23% | 74 → 30 |
| M5 control (editorial) | 2–13%, median 8% | 13–26% | 31–104 |

Skeleton share is now inside the clean-movement band for ch42, 43, 45 and 46, and above it for ch44 (16% against a band ceiling of 13%). Close-paraphrase share is still above the control band in ch42–45 (30–37% against 13–26%): many sentences are recognisable reworkings even where the skeleton rate is in band. Six-word runs sit at three to four times the clean chapters (ch40–41: 12–17), inside the control's range.

