### Scene by scene

**ch42 scene 3 (office, door, stair, aide) — new prose.** Own entry (Coss mid-sentence; Cael counting the room: four chairs, three taken, the aide on the landing bench), own business (the card put face down, the empty fourth chair facing all three, the clasp, Quenna counting the arrangements on her fingers on the half-landing), the "Coss had meant every word" orphan absorbed, the aide at the booth new. Coss's speech keeps the packet's order of points; its sentences are new. The ≥.50 hits are protected and brief-quoted lines plus three dialogue sentences. Residual: Quenna's second half-landing paragraph (80 words) still follows the source stair speech beat for beat — "I told a panel you'd be fact before anybody looked closely. You are fact. … He looked closely anyway. He thinks the category beats the fact. I put you in front of him, Cael. Whatever the next three weeks want from me, I'll give them." / source: "I staked my professional standing on the argument that you'd be established fact before anyone looked closely. You are established fact. … He looked closely anyway, and he's betting that fact loses to category. … I recruited you into this exposure, Cael. Whatever the next three weeks ask of me, they can have."

**ch42 scene 4 (family told) — frame rebuilt, speeches varied.** Brom's cups (seven high, and it did not lean), Lira's boots off at *field agent*, the Fenmark nineteen words (a true callback to ch24/26/34), Karis uncapping her pen "like somebody taking a blade off a rack" are the author's. But the three speeches — Lira's anger (110 words), Karis's war on paper (65), Brom's summary (62) — each follow the source speech sentence for sentence. Karis: "A hearing like this is a war fought on paper. Whoever reads the most, first, and keeps the best index, wins those. I have trained for that my whole life and for almost nothing else. … Whenever you find out what you need, you'll find I've started." / source: "A public adjudication hearing is a document war. Document wars are won by whoever has read more, sooner, with better indexing. I have spent my entire life training for exactly that and nothing else. … When you know what you need, I'll already have started." The packet fixes the content of all three speeches; it does not fix their sentence sequence. About 235 words of 661.

**ch43 scene 1 (notice and case) — new prose, with the source's beat order in its second half.** The clerk pressing the tacks home, the board as a date not a door, Prynn on her step putting a box away, "The runner talks. So does the kitchen." are new. From "This way" the beats run in the source's order (the case → the words never seen in ink → the stir of appetite at the rail → the three things → "the best thing anybody's said to me today" → "It usually is" → the first volume → the table where it always was → lock-up), about 410 words, with the sentences mostly rebuilt and two images still recognisable reworkings: "The old stir came up in him under the fear, the one he was half ashamed of, the one he had felt at the rail of the training hall in the second week looking down at eleven Paths he did not know. Appetite." / source: "He stood in front of it the way he had once stood at the rail of a training floor with eleven unfamiliar Paths moving below him — outmatched, outnumbered, and feeling, underneath the sensible fear, the old disgraceful stir of appetite." Acceptable; not a repair item.

**ch43 scenes 4–5 (council) — frame rebuilt, speeches varied.** Six people and five chairs with Brom standing; the challenge given a quarter turn; the clock ticking twice; the arithmetic said by Brom ("Two hundred against one"), Lira ("I've never been so angry at a number") and Cael ("So did I. In the archive.") in place of the source's essay paragraph; Naveth's hand on the challenge; the wager "with the house on fire"; the charter exchange. All new. But Naveth's three speeches (119, 74 and 95 words), Cael's third-way speech (152) and Naveth's ruling (152) each follow the corresponding source speech sentence for sentence, shortened. Naveth: "The gloss says a candidate who has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment. Cael has. We knew that when we signed him in. We made the category fit him because the other choice was leaving him with nowhere, and I would sign that form again this afternoon. … But I can't defend it. Counsel has spent two days trying. There is no defence of the enrollment itself that survives a challenge drafted this well, and this is the best-drafted thing anybody has ever sent me." / source: "The provision was written for children who have not Kindled. Cael has Kindled. … we built a fit for him because the alternative was leaving him with nothing, and I stand by that decision completely. But … my counsel has spent three days confirming there is no version of defending the enrollment itself that survives a competent challenge. Warden Coss's filing is competent. It may be the most competent thing that has ever crossed my desk." About 590 words of 1,762. The scene reads as the author's; the speeches read as the source's.

**ch44 scene 1 (division of labour; re-touch) — NOT RESOLVED.** The words changed in every paragraph (the paragraph map shows all of 2–18 altered) and the skeleton did not: 23% → 24%, the highest in the movement after the repair. Every speech (Cael, Quenna, Karis, Cael, Brom, Quenna, Brom, Naveth, Lira, Cael, Lira) and both narrative reflections follow source ch16's division scene in its order, sentence for sentence. Cael's reflection: "Of everything that had been laid on the desk that morning, he thought, this was what he needed most, and the one thing he would never have asked for. Naveth was guarding the academy, Karis was hunting the truth, and Brom was finding the witnesses. Lira was the only one guarding him: the boy under the case, against three weeks of argument, and against the kind of surviving where whatever survives isn't you any more." / source: "of everything anyone in the room had offered him that morning, this was the one he most needed and would have been least likely to ask for himself. Naveth was defending the enrollment. Karis was pursuing the truth. Brom was mustering the witnesses. Lira, alone of all of them, had assigned herself to defending *him* — the actual person, the boy under the case — against the argument itself, against the three weeks, against the version of survival that survived by becoming something else." Also Lira: "The yard at dusk, the same as always, and no excuses because you're busy being clever." / "Training floor, same as always, no excuses because you're busy being clever." Whole scene, ~900 words. This is the one scene that still counts as skeleton paraphrase under the brief's own test.

**ch44 scene 2 (breakup, Prynn's method, the tree, Karis with the lamp) — kept material as the brief allowed, two passages still track.** Prynn's method and the declaration tree were "keep" in the editorial review (16% then) and are unchanged in substance. The breakup paragraph is the editorial review's quoted ch44 example in varied form: "every one of them off to rearrange a life round something he had said in a room an hour ago" / "five people, redeploying their entire lives around a fifteen-year-old's insane sentence, because he had said it out loud and meant it", with the same five exits in the same order (~120 words). Karis's beat is re-set (out of the founding stacks with a lamp, not on the steps) but two of her three lines still track ("You keep saying that as though it might stop me." / "…as though it will discourage me."; the light seen twice before). Lighter item; see also §4 for the chronology defect this scene now carries.

**ch44 scene 4 (rules and scars) — entry rebuilt, body a shortened paraphrase in source order.** The forgotten slip, the strip of paper held out between two fingers, the roof stain "arguing since before you were born" are new and good. Then: the four rules in the source's order; the "one page that settles it" / "I'm not angry with it" / "Then what are you?" / "Studying it. As I'd study anybody I had to fight." / the pile to eleven high / "A fighter watches how a man moves before he worries where he's standing" exchange, line for line (195 words); the code's three ages and the scars, same three ages, same reasons, told shorter (322 words); and "It was two more days before it came to him that there was a third way a thing could go unscarred." / "It would take him two more days to learn that there was a third possibility." Example: "A defensive amendment was a scar, and each one meant that somebody had once found a soft place in the wording and pushed something through it hard enough to hurt." / "Every defensive amendment was a scar, and a scar was a record of a wound, and a wound meant that at some point, somewhere, somebody had found a gap in the text and driven something through it hard enough to make the Compact bleed." About 500 words of 821.

**ch45 scene 2 (four layers; re-touch) — RESOLVED.** Re-entered as a question put to his own tree ("what does this stand on?"), told once; P15 verbatim; the doubled "no scars" sentence removed, one left.

**ch45 scene 3 (the schedule; re-touch) — acceptable.** Skeleton 7%; sentences rebuilt; the beat order is the source's, which for a reading-the-schedule scene is close to the only order. The editorial review's quoted ch45 example persists in varied form: "There had to be a page he had missed, in this building or in a records hall three districts away: an amendment, an annex, an order somewhere that made the flag lawful. Nothing ran for centuries on nothing. But things did, and he had seen them do it." / "Somewhere in this building, or in some Compact records hall three districts away, there had to be the page he'd missed — the amendment, the annex, the enabling order that made the flag legal. Systems did not run for four centuries on nothing. Except that they did." Then the circuit, the market interview, fourteen, custom in law's coat, in the source's order (~177 words). Lighter item; note only.

**ch45 scene 4 (Prynn and the circled word) — frame rebuilt, the exchange varied.** The cold tea, Prynn reading the page upside down, "Turn it round", the cup poured away and brought back full, and the charter box checked afterwards are new and are the scene's best moments. But from "Is that a compliment?" to "Fourth shelf, second case" the exchange follows the source line for line, including Cael's summary of the argument (~510 words of 886): "Nobody within three districts would take it. And if they did, it would be theirs. The magistrate would hear a professional being clever, something paid for. It has to be me, or it sounds like a trick and not a reading." / "There's no lawyer within three districts who'd take it. And if there were, it would become the lawyer's argument. The panel would weigh it as a professional's maneuver — something purchased. It has to come from me, or it reads as a trick instead of a reading." And: "If somebody else stands up and says this on my behalf, then the one argument about nobody speaking for me gets made by somebody speaking for me." / "If someone else stands up in that room and argues this for me, then even the argument that the system never had the right to speak for me gets spoken for me."

**ch46 scene 2 opening (Lira's eighth-day drill) — acceptable.** The pea bag on the page, "halfway to being furniture", the cold cloth thrown to hit are new. Lira's drill paragraph still tracks ("She kept it old and short: first-year patterns, footwork his feet could have found asleep, the evasion frames from the Ardenmere yard before either of them had heard the word Greyvane." / "she kept them old — first-year patterns, footwork he could have run asleep, the evasion frames from before either of them had ever heard the word Greyvane."). One sentence; not a repair item.

**ch46 scene 5 (the outline and the four files) — first half new, second half tracks.** The page itself — *OUTLINE*, the fallback begun and struck through, the two margin notes, "No second argument. One argument. Built the way Brom stands.", the first sentence — is new and is the handle the editorial review asked for. From "It held four." the four-files and "how big it was" paragraphs (406 words of 878) follow source ch17 sentence for sentence: "If he won, he would not only keep one borrowed enrollment at one small academy. He would crack the machine, in public, on a record with his own name across the top. Every office that had ever expelled or watched or flagged anybody under that word would have to face, sooner or later, that a boy of fifteen had gone through their code with more care than any of them. And each official who faced it would learn who he was, because the record that kept the argument would keep whoever made it." / "If the argument won, it would not simply preserve one workaround enrollment at one minor academy. It would put a crack — public, on the record, with his name attached to it — directly into four hundred years of machinery … Every Compact office that had ever expelled, monitored, or flagged someone under that word would have to reckon, eventually, with the fact that a fifteen-year-old had read their own code more carefully than they had. And every official who reckoned with it would know his name, because the record that carried the argument would carry the arguer." Likewise the Vell sentence (0.60) and "Three of the files just stopped…" (0.47).

