None of the three is BOOK_MAP-protected; no later book depends on their wording. The brief's "every brief-quoted line stays exact" and the 8-word gate cannot both be satisfied for an 8-word source run without a `protected-patterns.txt` entry, which is an owner's call, not a defect. My preference: restore line 2 exactly with a pattern entry (`This is your enrollment, not mine to trade`), keep 1 and 3 as they are. Not required for close.

Related: Prynn's §9.2 line now reads `She said, "Sixty years I've kept these shelves. You're the first person…"`. The words are verbatim; the "She said," in front is the only such construction in the movement and shows the seam. The author's reason (keeping the whole run inside the protected text) was unnecessary: `"Sixty years I've kept these shelves," said Prynn. "You're the first person to read the code the way it's written instead of the way it's enforced."` passes the gate too (the first fragment is six words; the second lies inside the BOOK_MAP line). Optional fix below.

