books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:6:**Companions introduced: none — the roster is complete through Book 6; this is Seln's first full book AS a companion rather than becoming one**
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:28:- [ ] **Book 5 delivery (this book):** The three individual companion matches land exactly as the series bible specifies, each as a real arc with real page-time, not summary: Lira vs. a speed specialist who outpaces her (Zerin, Wind Path, Iron Rank 9, Auremont — Chapter 10, a full featured chapter); Brom vs. a Glass Path striker who finds every gap in his defense (Marek, Glass Path, Copper Rank 1, Rhagen — Chapter 11, a full featured chapter); Karis vs. an information-advantage opponent who knows her technique perfectly (Ivenne, Ember Path, Iron Rank 2, Ternhall — her former training partner — Chapter 12, a full featured chapter). Each companion fights separately BEFORE the companions fight together: the individual brackets (Ch9–13) precede the team trial (Ch15 semifinal, Ch17 third-place trial). The order is structural, per the bible, and must not be reshuffled in drafting.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:29:- [ ] **Book 5 delivery (this book — the main event):** Cael vs. Daeva — 19 years old, Gold-tier Rank 3, the youngest Gold-tier practitioner in continental history (Storm Path, Auremont; the Path is PROVISIONAL, chosen here — the bible specifies only her name, age, and rank). Chapters 20–21, two full chapters of choreography. Cael does not win — Daeva takes the match on stoppage — and what he demonstrates has never been recorded in tournament history: integration of an opponent's ability mid-fight and immediate deployment, live, in front of judges. The judges do not know how to score it (Chapter 22: the panel returns "performance unscorable under standard" — the tournament's own instrument failing on the record). The match record becomes the most-reviewed result in tournament history. NOTE on SECRET discipline: what goes public is the EFFECT (a practitioner with no Storm record deploying Storm architecture mid-bout), never the MECHANISM. The word "integration," the fragment notices, the acquisition conditions — none of it is spoken, written, or guessed correctly by anyone outside the inner circle. The public record holds an inexplicable event, not an explanation. Book 6's threat-classification machinery runs on exactly that gap.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:33:- [ ] **Book 6 seed (maintain, do not advance):** Seln's cache and his null reports. His reporting continues through the qualifying year in the established pattern — true, complete in form, empty. The tournament then does something strange to the pattern, and the book notes it in one window (Ch22): for the first time since midwinter of Book 4, his report writes itself, because the whole continent watched — there is nothing left to omit about the Daeva match, and the window catches his odd, unexamined relief at filing a true report that costs nothing. The locked case travels to Norhold and back unopened. Its contents are never shown, never hinted. Vastin does NOT detect the null-report pattern — that thread remains reserved, per Book 4's checkpoint.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:35:- [ ] **LOCKED — must stay open:** The Tide-adjacent anomaly (Book 2, session nine). Log-inventory references only — Chapter 14 and Chapter 24, the standing three words ("Still open. Still real. Patience."). NOT reproduced, NOT confirmed, NOT advanced. Fragment count math everywhere in this book treats it as an anomaly entry, never an eighth fragment. Drafting discipline in support: NO Tide Path practitioner appears on-page anywhere in this book — the tournament's cast is deliberately built without one, so the anomaly is never handed an acquisition-adjacent temptation before its time.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:36:- [ ] **LOCKED — untouched threads:** The patient Iron Skin watcher (B2 Ch15), the Book 1 market stranger, Hesk's full history (letter beats only), Vell's offered archive session (unclaimed; at most a letter reference), Coss's exact grade (Coss himself is offstage this book — at most one passing mention). Reydan's "find me later — I want the answer" is touched in exactly one log line (Ch24: "Reydan will read the accounts. It still isn't the answer. Not yet.") and not advanced.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:37:- [ ] **Book 4 minor thread (advanced and substantially closed):** The records-broker's client (B4 Ch13). Chapter 5: Seln, running counter-scouting on the qualifying circuit, matches the broker's tasking pattern to a commercial scouting house whose Cael dossier is now a product — sold to academies, bettors, and anyone with the fee. Conclusion, logged in Seln's dry register: *private interest confirmed — the tournament economy, not the Compact.* The original commissioning client's name is unrecoverable and no longer matters; the thread closes as benign, with one residue deliberately kept: the dossier itself now circulates, which means every opponent Cael ever faces has read a version of him. Filed as texture, not debt.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:46:**The Silver Standard (the title's referent — an object and an argument, per the Copper Crown precedent):** Two things wearing one name. First, the instrument: the tournament's normalization benchmark — a fixed reference performance profile, calibrated to a Silver-tier Rank 5 baseline, against which judge panels scale every scored performance in every bracket so that a Copper bout and a Gold bout can feed the same team standings. It is, by general agreement, the fairest measuring device the continent has ever built: it does not care what academy you came from, what your family paid, or what your paper says — only how you perform against the fixed mark. Second, the banner: the physical standard, silver on grey, carried by the reigning champion academy and surrendered at each cycle's close. The book's argument in one object: the fairest instrument ever built is still an instrument — and the year's whole story is what happens when the fairest instrument on the continent is pointed at Cael and returns nothing at all.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:49:- *Individual tier brackets* — Copper, Iron, Silver, Gold — seeded by formal tier (the Book 4 cage at continental scale: the brackets sort by paper, and the paper is still wrong about exactly the people this series follows).
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:56:- *Zerin:* Auremont. Wind Path, Iron-tier Rank 9. Speed specialist; Kindled at twelve, continental training every year since — the Lira the system built right, which is precisely why the match is the arc-test the bible orders. Female, economical, without malice.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:58:- *Ivenne:* Ternhall Academy. Ember Path, Iron-tier Rank 2 — Ternhall's current top rank, holding the standing Karis vacated when she transferred to Greyvane. Three years of shared training floor with Karis; knows her lattice, her tells, her documentation habits, and has Ternhall's full scouting file besides. The information-advantage opponent as a person Karis used to trust.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:59:- *Academies in attendance:* **Auremont** (continental-tier, reigning champion, Daeva's and Zerin's), **Rhagen Institute** (striker doctrine, Marek's), **Ternhall** (Karis's former school), **Fenmark** (Lira's former school — present, background, and paid off in Chapter 6), Halcenvane, and the field.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:62:**The Halcenvane delegation:** Withrow (who has aimed her institution at this for three years and says so), Rooke (team coach — his cohort's year), Gault (evaluator of record; the assay provision's semester machinery travels with its enrollee), Seln (assessment-office staff — the perfect legitimate reason to be everywhere), and the team: **Brom** (Copper bracket), **Lira** (Iron bracket), **Karis** (Iron bracket), **Ephram** (Iron bracket), and **Cael** — on the roster because the roster is the enrollment record, and the enrollment record is the law.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:75:The summer recess, staged in the log-voice opening movement the way Book 4 staged its skip — dated, inventoried, done: three months of training on a quieter campus; letters (Hesk's arriving fast as ever); the watchers outside the walls holding their rotation with the patience of men paid by the year; Seln at his desk the whole recess, which nobody remarks on and everybody notices; Karis spending the break doing what Karis does with a banked clause — building the argument around it until the argument can stand cross-examination. Cael is sixteen, and says so on the page. Six confirmed fragments. One anomaly. One semester stamp. The book opens on the first day of the qualifying year.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:77:Withrow's convocation address makes it official: Halcenvane will contest the Continental Academy Tournament's qualifying season — the program she has been building since before Cael arrived, now with a formally Iron-tier Wind champion, a repaired Iron Skin cohort standout, an Iron R3 Ember researcher, and the most-watched enrollee on the continent. The chapter's centerpiece is the roster submission itself, staged as quiet procedure with a loaded chamber: the tournament requires each academy's full enrollment record; Bracken prepares it; and Cael's name goes onto the qualifying roster not by anyone's daring but by the record being what the record is. Karis, watching the courier leave with the documents, says the quiet thing: "We didn't put you on the roster. We just declined to commit fraud by leaving you off. The clause does the rest." Cael, who spent Book 3 learning what text can hold, turns the sentence over and finds it load-bearing.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:97:The first qualifying meet, and the five tracks in braid — the Book 4 Chapter 3 architecture run at competitive altitude. Lira's Iron-bracket debut is what a year of formal tier finally permits: real opponents, on the record, no cage — she wins her opening bouts with the not-holding-back style now sharpened by Rooke's program, and discovers that being priced right is its own strange weight ("Nobody gets to be surprised by me anymore. I have to actually be this good"). Brom, back in the Copper bracket the paper still insists on, wins with the heavy patience of a man solving a familiar cage — but the chapter notes what Book 4 noted: continental Copper is not Halcenvane Copper, and the qualifying field has students the system mispriced in both directions. Karis treats qualifying as fieldwork — her bouts are efficient and her notebook grows a tournament section. Ephram, Iron bracket, is very good and knows it, and is beginning to be watchable for reasons other than rank.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:99:Cael's track is the strange one, and the chapter stages the mechanics the whole book runs on: he cannot be seeded, so the meet hosts schedule him in demonstration exhibitions — the fossil category, dusted off, opponents by volunteer challenge. At the first meet the volunteers are curious locals: an Iron-tier who read the dossier and wants the story, a Bronze-tier with something to prove. Cael runs them the way Vell's circuit taught him — conservative, complete, nothing the record doesn't hold — and wins with a control that reads, to anyone with eyes, as a practitioner operating well inside his margins.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:108:The Silver Standard meets Cael — the instrument-and-boy chapter, and the arc statement's first pole, written in confidence and destined to be wrong. Exhibition bouts are scored for performance rating against the Standard's baseline, and the chapter walks the reader through the scoring architecture with the same care Book 3 gave registry law: judge panels rate execution, control, and effect against the fixed Silver-tier reference profile; the rating scales into meet standings. For every other practitioner on the continent, the Standard is a mirror. For Cael it is a mask he chooses the fit of: he calibrates his exhibitions to score *exactly* legible — the banking doctrine's tournament form, showing precisely what the instrument expects prepared for, in the shape it can rate. It works. It works so well it is quietly disturbing: his qualifying performance ratings land in a tidy band just above Iron, exactly where a careful anomaly would want them.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:112:*Key beat (the first pole, on the page):* the Power Log, after the meet: "Rated again. In-band again. The judges keep writing numbers that mean 'strong Iron, ceiling unknown,' and the honest entry is: I can beat anyone at my tier. I've tested that sentence for three years and it holds. What I haven't tested is what tier means when the word points at me. Not this season's problem." He is wrong about that last line, and the book knows it.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:119:The tournament's shadow-industry chapter — and Seln's, in the quiet register the series has committed to for him. Qualifying season draws the scouting economy the way a harvest draws markets: academy scouts, betting-house handicappers, and commercial scouting houses that compile and sell dossiers on anyone worth a fee. Cael, who spent Book 2 learning that information asymmetry works both ways, watches his own principle industrialized: at the second meet, Brom is handed a rival academy's purchased dossier on himself, four pages, accurate to his Book 3 flaw and out of date on its repair — which is its own useful information.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:121:Seln's window, mid-chapter, runs the counter-scouting the delegation never formally asked him for: he acquires the circulating Cael dossier through channels the book does not detail, reads it professionally, and finds inside it a tasking fingerprint he has seen before — the same pattern as the records-office broker from the Halcenvane intrusion (B4 Ch13). The conclusion lands flat, the way his conclusions do: the broker's client was the scouting economy itself; the dossier commissioned then is the ancestor of the product for sale now; *private interest, confirmed — the market, not the Compact.* He logs it, tells the inner circle in his oblique administrative register, and the Book 4 thread closes at its natural size. The residue stays deliberately live: every opponent from here forward has read a version of Cael. "Good," is Cael's whole response, which makes Seln look at him for a second longer than usual. "They'll prepare for the version in the file. I'd rather fight people who prepared for the wrong me than people who prepared for nothing."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:123:*Close on:* the null-report beat, one paragraph, maintained: Seln's quarterly filing renders the season *routine; subject's competitive activity conducted within enrollment basis; no undisclosed capability observed.* True. Complete in form. Empty.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:130:Lira's past, paid off at exactly the size it deserves — a qualifying meet hosted in Fenmark's region, with Fenmark's delegation on the floor. The chapter is built to be smaller than the reader fears: no confrontation, no speech, no institutional reckoning. Lira walks into a hall wearing the academy colors of a school that priced her right, with a formal Iron-tier certification the school that threw her away said she'd never hold, and discovers — mid-warmup, undramatically — that Fenmark has become a fact about other people. Her bout that day is against a Fenmark Iron-bracket entrant who was two years behind her and plainly knows exactly who she is; she wins it cleanly, shakes the hand, and files the whole institution under done. "I kept thinking I'd want a moment," she tells Cael that night. "Turns out the moment was three years of work, and I already had it."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:132:The chapter's forward motion: the scouting sheets. Rooke circulates the continental Iron-bracket profiles for the finals field, and one page stops Lira cold — **Zerin**, Auremont, Wind Path, Iron Rank 9, with meet times that should not be possible at Iron. Lira reads the sheet three times, the way Cael reads notices. "She's not faster than me," she says finally, and the room waits. "She's faster than me *right now*." It is the most honest sentence her arc has ever produced, and the match it aims at is nine chapters away. *Secondary thread:* Brom's page of the continental Copper profiles includes Marek of Rhagen — a Copper Rank 1 whose bout notations read like surgical reports. Brom studies the page with the expression he once reserved for Cael. "This one's been mispriced too," he says. "The dangerous direction."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:139:The qualifying season's close — Halcenvane qualifies, and the book's institutional weather shifts one degree colder. The final meet is the delegation's best showing: Lira takes the regional Iron bracket, Brom the Copper, Karis and Ephram place, and Cael's exhibitions draw the largest demonstration-category crowds the regional circuit has ever logged (the fossil category, the officials note with some bemusement, is suddenly the hottest ticket in the qualifying year). Halcenvane's standing clears the continental threshold with a meet to spare. Withrow's address to the team is one sentence: "Now we go be measured by everyone at once."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:154:The Part-ending hook: the draw posts at dusk on the Concourse boards, and the chapter closes on the four of them reading it in the torchlight. Lira's Iron pool: Zerin in the semifinal line. Brom's Copper bracket: Marek seeded first. Karis's Iron pool: Ternhall — Ivenne — in the quarterfinal line. And at the foot of the exhibition schedule, a blank where Cael's opponents will go, because the volunteer category doesn't draw — it waits. Cael reads the blank longer than the names. "Everyone else knows who they're fighting," he says. "I'm about to find out who volunteers."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:167:The early rounds run: Lira and Karis through their opening Iron bouts (separate pools — the chapter confirms the draw's geometry on-page so the reader never wonders why they don't meet), Brom cutting through Copper openers, Ephram solid into the Iron round of sixteen. Cael's exhibition slate posts with two volunteer challenges — both Silver-tier, both curious, scheduled for Chapter 13 — and the Concourse's rumor engine celebrates: the demonstration category hasn't drawn Silver volunteers in living memory. *Key beat (banked, quietly):* Karis, reading the team-trial rules in preparation for the squad phase, stops on the eligibility text — *squads shall be drawn from the academy's roster* — no tier qualifier, no classification requirement. She reads it twice, looks at nothing in particular, and copies it into the notebook beside the fielding clause with one word: "Again." (Not acted on. Not yet. Chapter 15's premise, banked exactly as the Book 4 note was banked.)
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:176:Lira's chapter — the first of the bible's three individual arc-tests, given its full featured build. The structure honors her series-long spine: the chapter opens on her quarterfinal (won, efficient, one paragraph) and then builds the Zerin semifinal the way Book 2 built Reydan — study, hypothesis, and the honest terror of the scouting sheet. Zerin's times aren't propaganda. Lira and Cael break down her bout records together, the old partnership running its oldest pattern, and the finding is the one Lira already named: no seam, no trick — just six more years of doors. Wind Path trained continentally since age twelve, every advancement on schedule, every resource on time. "I'm not fighting her," Lira says, taping her wrists the night before. "I'm fighting the version of me they were supposed to build. Fine. Let's see what the scrapyard version does."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:178:**The match, full choreography.** First exchange: outpaced — cleanly, structurally, the way the bible orders it. Zerin's speed isn't a burst; it's a *baseline*, and Lira's circuit-honed reads arrive a tenth late all exchange. She loses it going away and adjusts. Second exchange: Lira stops racing and starts *spending* — economy against velocity, the Vell-era doctrine of making every motion buy two things, forcing Zerin to run her speed through corners Lira chooses. She takes the exchange, and the Concourse notices: nobody in the bracket has taken an exchange off Zerin all tournament. Third and fourth: the margin tells. Zerin, continental-trained, has answers Lira's improvisation can't outspend twice; she solves the corner-game in the third and closes the fourth at full baseline. Lira loses the semifinal on points — extended further than anyone at Norhold extends her, and beaten anyway, in public, at the tier her certification finally says she belongs to.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:180:**Aftermath (the arc beat, staged with care):** Zerin finds her at the boards. "Fenmark threw you away?" Lira: "Their ledger said Copper." Zerin, flatly: "Their ledger is garbage. You cornered me twice with no continental training. That's not a Copper anything." A beat. "Silver bracket, next cycle. Be there." Lira walks back to the delegation floor, sits down next to Cael the way Brom once sat down next to him, and says the thing her whole arc has been rebuilding toward: "I used to want them to admit what I already was. Now I want to become something they haven't seen yet. It's a better want. It costs more." Her Silver trajectory — the want the system will eventually make her choose against (B6) — is now on the page, in her voice.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:189:**The match.** First exchange: the bible's sentence, staged literally — Marek finds every gap. The repaired force-redirect holds (Book 4's work, honored), so Marek simply goes around it: shoulder seam, timing seam, the half-beat where Iron Skin's amplification resets. He scores five times in the first exchange, none heavy, all precise, and the Concourse understands it is watching a demonstration of doctrine. Second exchange: Brom does the thing that has made him Brom since Book 2 — he stops defending the gaps and starts *pricing* them. Every gap Marek takes now costs a contact Brom chooses, Iron Skin amplification pre-loaded at the point Marek must pass through to reach the seam. He loses the exchange on points and wins it on information. Third and fourth: the accumulation. Marek is landing more and carrying more; Glass doctrine assumes the opponent protects what it finds, and Brom has inverted the assumption — he is spending his own gaps like currency, and the redirect returns are compounding. Marek adapts twice; the third structure he cannot solve before the accumulated cost slows the finding. Brom ends it in the fourth, a full-amplification redirect through a gap he left open on purpose from the first exchange — the longest con of his fighting life.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:191:**Brom takes the continental Copper title.** The ceremony beat is deliberately rhymed with Lira's Crown: he holds it like evidence. The consequences file in behind: the title triggers a mandatory advancement review — scheduled post-tournament, PENDING at book's close, not resolved (the ledger the Book 6 walkout will cost him, staged here). And one small envelope, Velmere-sealed, delivered to the delegation floor that evening. He opens it alone; the chapter shows one line, in his sister's hand: *"Grandmother watched the lists for your name. She found it."* He reads it twice, files it in his kit beside nothing else like it, and comes to dinner. "Anything?" Lira asks. "Noted," says Brom, which from Brom is a whole letter back.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:198:Karis's chapter — the third arc-test, and the series' title-rhyme tradition honored (What Fenmark Kept; What Seln Keeps). The Ivenne match is built as the information war it is: Ternhall's file on Karis is three years deep, and its author — for practical purposes — is Ivenne, who shared her training floor, learned her lattice beside her, and holds the top rank Karis vacated. The pre-match chapters of study invert comically and then painfully: Karis, the series' great preparer, discovers there is nothing to prepare — Ivenne already knows all of it. Every tell Ternhall trained *in* (standardization is legibility — her own Book 3 line, returned to sender), every lattice habit, every documented preference. Cael names the real problem the night before, because he has lived it from the other side since Book 2: "You can't out-study someone who studied *with* you. You have to be someone the file hasn't met." Karis, slowly: "The file is accurate." Cael: "The file is *finished*. You're not."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:200:**The match.** First exchange: the file wins. Ivenne pre-empts the lattice before it closes, counters ignition points before they ignite — fighting Karis's documentation, flawlessly. Second exchange: Karis does the hardest thing her arc has ever asked — she abandons her own method mid-bout. No lattice. No documented structure. She fights the way she has spent three books *watching other people fight*: Lira's economy, Brom's pricing, the circuit's not-holding-back, deployed through Ember architecture in configurations she has never written down because she invented them between exchanges. Ivenne's information advantage doesn't degrade — it *inverts*: every pre-emption aimed at the documented Karis is a commitment the improvising one punishes. Third exchange: Ivenne, to her credit, tries to adapt to the stranger in her friend's colors, and runs out of bout to do it in. Karis wins the quarterfinal — then drops the semifinal two days later to Auremont's lattice-breaker in a brief, honest paragraph (extended, outmatched, content).
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:211:The matches, in the series' choreography register: Cael wins both — and pays for the first honestly enough that the second is watched in a different quality of silence. Silver is not a bigger Iron: the Shield captain's declarations carry a *depth* — sustained architecture under sustained load — that no amount of tier-lateral cleverness fully answers, and Cael solves him with margin thinner than the crowd can see and thinner than Cael expected. The banking doctrine holds — public suite only, in-band deployment, nothing new surfaced — but the log entry after the first match is the pivot the book is built around, the first pole cracking: "I said I can beat anyone at my tier. I just beat Silver. So which tier is mine? Iron said no. Silver said no, slower. The sentence I've been standing on assumes the ladder includes a rung with my name on it. Tonight's honest entry: I have never once stood on the ladder. I've been climbing alongside it, and calling the nearest rung mine."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:220:The rest-day chapter — the tournament's pressure valve, and the series' second staged birthday, deliberately smaller than the first because the first did the structural work and this one only has to be true. Norhold's mid-tournament rest day: the delegation scattered, the Concourse quiet. Lira — instigator emerita — assembles the table at a dockside cookshop; Brom acquires food with quiet competence; Karis brings nothing and writes nothing, which from Karis is a gift; and Seln — the beat the whole series has been walking toward at his pace, not the reader's — comes to the table and *sits down at it*. Nobody makes anything of it. That is the point of it. One step past the Book 4 wall, taken the way he takes everything: without announcement.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:222:Hesk's package, held ready as always: a bound blank ledger volume, instrument-maker's work, and the note in full: *"Seventeen. My trade says an instrument is finished when it measures true — not when it looks done. You measure true. Let the rest of them look. — H."* Cael is seventeen, on the page, witnessed, the date written into the log in his own hand. The inventory follows in the new volume's first entry: six confirmed fragments — Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow — deployment notes current; one anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over once, closed with the standing three words: *Still open. Still real. Patience.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:224:*The chapter's forward motion:* the team trial draw posts at dusk. Halcenvane's semifinal opponent: **Auremont**. The table goes quiet in the specific way tables go quiet when the draw says the thing everyone hoped and feared. Karis lays the squad-eligibility clause on the table — *drawn from the roster*, no tier qualifier — and looks at Withrow's empty chair, then at Cael. "You're on the roster," she says. "The clause does the rest. Again." *Close on:* the Power Log: "Seventeen. Six fragments. Tomorrow I find out if the second technicality holds, and if it does — the found family fights together, on a sanctioned floor, for the first time in any of our lives. Whatever else this tournament is for, it was for that."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:231:The companions fight together — the bible's structural promise, first installment, staged after all three individual arcs per the locked order. The eligibility beat resolves fast and in-world: Withrow files the squad list with Cael's name; Umber's office, already scarred by the fielding clause, reads the trial rules, finds no tier qualifier, and — in the chapter's best institutional grace-note — approves it with visible relief, because the trial is scored on unit performance against the Standard and the adjudication office would rather rate him inside a system than improvise another category. Halcenvane fields: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Ephram. Rooke coaches. The Concourse sells out.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:233:**The semifinal vs. Auremont, full unit choreography.** The scenario floor: objective control, engagement scoring, five on five. The chapter's first half is the payoff of four books of found-family mechanics — the compound gaze feeding Lira's economy, Brom pricing lanes, Karis's lattice sealing what Brom prices, Ephram — fully integrated, his own arc quietly completing — running point like Rooke's best product. They are, for two exchanges, the better *unit* on the floor. And then Auremont answers with the thing units cannot price: Daeva. Her stormlane opens the third exchange, and the trial's geometry collapses — she is not better than five people, she is *orthogonal* to them, a Gold-tier fact moving through an Iron-tier argument. The first Cael–Daeva contact happens here, held to a single exchange by design: her lane crosses his position, he reads the pressure differential a half-second early (Iron-adjacent surface read, at its limit), survives the crossing that should have scored, and for one full second, mid-trial, Daeva stops — and looks at him the way Cael looks at things. Auremont takes the semifinal on accumulated objective points. Halcenvane exits to the third-place trial.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:244:Then the filing: **Daeva, Auremont, Gold-tier Rank 3, files a voluntary demonstration challenge against the unclassified Halcenvane enrollee.** The chapter runs the detonation through every register the book has built: Umber's office in genuine crisis (Gold-versus-unclassified has no precedent, no insurance category, and no scoring guidance — the Standard's normalization tables literally do not extend to a Gold-tier exhibition against an unrated opponent); Auremont's delegation formally objecting to its own student's filing and being ignored by her; Withrow running the calculus she has run since Book 4 Chapter 2 and landing where ambition and law agree ("The clause put him on the roster. The category exists. She filed. Denying it now would require someone to write down *why* — and nobody who could write it wants their name on it"); and the inner circle's council, where the vote that matters is Cael's alone and everyone knows it. Seln's contribution is one sentence, delivered as scheduling trivia: "The adjudication office has requested three additional recording scribes for finals day." Translation, which the whole table performs silently: whatever happens in that match will be the most-documented event of the tournament. Cael accepts on the record the next morning.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:257:The third-place trial vs. Rhagen — the companions' together-victory, staged BEFORE the main event and deliberately so: the tournament's official ledger closes on Halcenvane before the most important match of the cycle, which is worth zero points, and the book wants the reader to feel the absurdity as architecture. The trial itself is the found family's full-page payoff: Rhagen fields a doctrine squad built around Marek and the Shield captain from Chapter 13, and Halcenvane beats them as a *unit* — the chapter's choreography is deliberately ensemble, no single hero exchange, the five of them running two books of shared floor-time like a language. Brom prices Marek for a second time and the second time is faster; Lira spends Zerin's lesson on Rhagen's speed line; Karis's undocumented improvisation is now, one match later, a *style*; Ephram captains the objective game; and Cael — fielded, legal, and visibly the reason the unit's reads run a half-second early — plays exactly in-band, banking doctrine intact, one match away from the fight where banking dies.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:259:**Halcenvane places third in the Continental Academy Tournament.** The standings land on-page with the math the season built: Brom's Copper title, Lira's Iron semifinal, Karis's quarterfinal run, Ephram's round of sixteen, team trial third. For a mid-tier academy three years into Withrow's program, it is a triumph with no asterisk — the chapter gives the delegation its night, whole and earned, before the book turns to the match the standings can't hold. Withrow's toast is in her flattest registry-official register and lands like a flag planted: "Third on the continent, by the instrument's own arithmetic. Whatever else happens on finals day — they counted us. Remember when they wouldn't."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:268:The window chapter — the series' limited third-person introduction technique (Coss B1, Brom B2, Karis B3, Seln B4, Vastin B4), deployed for the last major figure of the arc, and written as the mirror the book has promised. Daeva's window: Kindled at eleven — a year early, the earliest verified Kindling of her generation — Storm Path, a rare dual-aspect architecture the registry celebrated as much as it recorded. Gold at seventeen; Gold Rank 3 at nineteen; the youngest in continental history at each threshold. The window's work is the cost accounting nobody at Norhold performs: she has been the system's favorite anomaly since before she understood the word — accelerated cohorts, exhibition schedules, her Kindling anniversary a minor Auremont holiday — and she has not fought an opponent whose outcome was in genuine doubt in four years. The system built a ladder to her exact height and then congratulated her for standing on it. Her window's one unguarded note, the thesis of the mirror: she read the Cael dossier in one sitting, twice, and what she felt was not curiosity. It was *recognition* — the only other practitioner of her generation the instruments talk about instead of measure. She filed the challenge the next morning. Auremont objected. She is nineteen, Gold Rank 3, and it was, she reflects without drama, the first decision of her entire career that nobody scheduled for her.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:277:The night-before chapter — the fighter's ritual, series tradition (B3 Ch20 gifts, B4 Ch20 strategy), given its third form: this time what the companions bring is neither gifts nor calibration, because the calibration problem has inverted. The study session first, honest about its limits: there is no film on Gold — Auremont litigates its students' bout records into proprietary silence, Storm Path documentation is registry-sealed, and the only primary data is the team-trial exchange and Cael's own compound-gaze notes from the gallery. Karis builds the model anyway (stormlane mechanics: pressure differential, corridor collapse, discharge timing — hypothesis: the lane must be *seeded* a half-beat before entry; if a tell exists, it is atmospheric, not somatic). Brom's contribution is the veteran's frame: "You've never fought anyone where survival was the win condition. That's the actual match. Score is for the judges." Lira's is her Book 3 line, third issue, now liturgy: "The person who walks onto that floor has to still be you."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:279:The strategic heart of the chapter is the banking decision, staged as the formal end of an era: surviving Gold requires everything, and Cael — with the inner circle, on the page, in the log — spends the bank. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember: full public deployment authorized for the first time in his life. One reserve held, deliberately, and the holding *named*: Shadow-adjacent stays sealed — not because it would not help, but because it is the one fragment whose exposure burns someone else's cover, and the debt he owes its source does not suspend for a Gold match. (Seln is not told he is the reason. The asymmetry-debt beat, carried forward from B4 Ch14, honored and still unpaid.) Seln's actual contribution arrives as tradecraft, unsigned, in the coursework-folder register of B4 Ch20: *Judges rewatch what they cannot score. Whatever you show tomorrow will be studied frame by frame by people whose profession is categories. Decide tonight what you are content to have studied forever.* Cael burns the note. Then he sits with the log a long time and writes the entry the arc requires: "Quenna asked if I could not-do it in front of people who wish me harm. I learned to. Tomorrow's question is new: whether I can afford to keep not-doing it in front of someone who can kill me by accident. I don't think I can. Write it down before, not after."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:288:**First exchange:** the gulf, staged honestly. Daeva opens at what the crowd believes is speed and Cael's Iron-adjacent read tells him is *courtesy* — a calibration pass, Gold-tier professionalism, finding his ceiling so she can fight just above it. Even her courtesy outpaces anything he has faced: the stormlane's pressure differential arrives before she does, his evasion framework reads the lane a half-beat late, and only the full suite — Wind evasion stacked on Compression absorption, spent openly for the first time ever — keeps the exchange survivable. The Concourse sees the unclassified enrollee's full public repertoire in ninety seconds and understands it is watching him *lose ground*. **Second exchange:** Cael solves the seeding tell — Karis's model holds; the lane telegraphs atmospherically, a pressure-fold a half-beat before entry — and begins arriving early instead of late. He scores once, contact-range, Ember ignition against the lane's collapse point, and the crowd sound changes: nobody at Norhold has scored on Daeva in four years. She recalibrates upward. **Third exchange:** the real Gold. Multiple lanes, seeded in sequence, discharge timed to punish exactly the early arrivals his adaptation just taught her to expect — the prodigy's information game running at a speed even the compound gaze pays for. He survives it spending everything, takes discharge damage along the left side that his frameworks only partially disperse, and the exchange ends with the honest arithmetic on the floor for everyone to read: he has solved her twice, she has solved him three times, and the match's remaining length is a number she controls.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:297:The match, conclusion — the chapter the book exists for. **Fourth exchange:** Daeva ends the courtesy. Full-baseline stormlane work, the corridor geometry that took the team trial apart, and Cael's suite — brilliant, spent, Iron-equivalent at its ceiling — begins failing in sequence. The chapter stages his position with total clarity: out of margin, out of adaptations, one exchange from stoppage, and inside the most complete acquisition conditions the architecture has ever been offered — sustained observation at survival intensity, ultimate stakes, an ability in earnest adversarial use against him at close range. The Chapter 9-of-Book-4 line, drawn in advance and stood on ever since, permits exactly this. He reaches. Directed. Open-eyed. Mid-fight.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:301:**The cost, live, and the honest ending:** the destabilization arrives mid-ring — Gold-intensity architecture landing on frameworks already spent, the cascade the Book 3 constraint has always promised, at match speed. The Wind framework drops mid-motion; the new fragment pulls at everything around it; Cael's next step is a stumble the whole Concourse sees. Daeva reads the collapse in half a second and does the most professional thing in the book: she ends it — one controlled lane, one measured pin, no discharge — precise, proportionate, almost gentle. **Stoppage. Daeva.** Umber's voice into the silence is the only sound. The notice arrives while Cael is still on the reinforced floor:
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:321:*Plant (Book 6 — advanced, not resolved):* Ilsev, in the Compact observation office, processing the referral Umber's entry generates — and finding, in the same evening's dispatch case, the return on her own second referral from Book 4: *referral closed at registry level; no further inquiry authorized.* The chapter gives her reaction one paragraph and makes it count: a career textualist knows the difference between a question the system cannot answer and a question the system has forbidden, and she has just been handed her first of the second kind. She does the thing she has never done in five books: writes a private note. Havel, delivering the dispatch case, watches her write it, and that night adds a fifth entry to his own. Two carriers, both now off the record. Nobody reaches the truth. LOCKED. *Seln's window, one paragraph, the null-report inversion:* his match report writes itself — public record, eight thousand witnesses, nothing to omit — and the window catches his unexamined relief at filing a true report that protects no one because no report could. He files it. He updates the private file anyway. Habit. *Close on:* Cael and the arc statement's second pole, written at the rail above the empty exhibition floor: "They didn't score me low. They didn't score me high. They couldn't score me. I have spent three years telling myself I can beat anyone at my tier. Tonight the fairest instrument ever built agreed with the Arbiter, the brackets, and the registry: **I am not at any tier.** I keep waiting for that sentence to feel like a wound. It feels like the truth arriving."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:328:The Vastin conversation — the book's antagonist-track keystone and the series' first honest institutional warning, staged with the discipline the bible's constraint demands: civil throughout, threatening never, and heavier than any threat the series has staged. The mechanism: a request through Withrow's office, two days after the match, before the delegations disperse — explicitly informal, no record, no counsel, no aides, a borrowed tournament administration office as neutral ground. The companions object to the format; Cael overrules them, alone, and the chapter marks the growth: at fifteen he insisted on a witness for Coss. At seventeen he has read this man across two books, and what he has read is worth meeting on its own terms. Seln's only comment, which Cael carries in: "That man has kept every rule of a forty-year career. Whatever he wants to say off the record cost him more than you can see. Listen accordingly."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:332:*Aftermath:* the inner-circle council, that night, the warning on the table. Karis maps it against the record (the closed referral, the stopped queries, the convening rumors already moving through the Compact observation staff); Seln delivers the professional verdict the reader needs weighed: "Wardens threaten. Politicians hint. That was neither. That was an honest man spending career capital to tell you the truth a few months early. Treat the warning as real, because it is." *Close on:* the log: "First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:341:The ledger of the year, in its established forms. The Power Log inventory, in Hesk's new volume: **seven confirmed fragments** — Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent, Ember-adjacent, Shadow-adjacent, Storm-adjacent, the last still flagged *stability: provisional*, its integration cost logged honestly across the post-match week (the frameworks resettling slower than after any acquisition before it; Gold architecture is heavy) — and the anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over once, the standing three words. One log line for an old promise: "Reydan will read the accounts. It still isn't the answer. Not yet." Letters: to Hesk (whose reply, fast as ever, reads in full: *"Third on the continent, and the judges wrote 'no number goes here.' Your grandmother would have had that framed. Seventeen, unmeasured, and true. Come home when the road allows. — H."*); to Vell, because her ledger still holds his first honest record and she will want the tournament told straight. Brom's advancement review: scheduled, pending, unresolved on the page. Lira, at the rail, watching Auremont's banner: "Silver bracket. Next cycle." Nobody at the rail says what the reader and Vastin already suspect about whether the official channels will last that long. The road home from Norhold: the six of them — Seln traveling with the delegation, at the table now, which no one remarks on and everyone counts.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:345:The last lines, matched to the series' pattern: "I have seven things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "a match record no instrument can hold, and five people who watched them fail to score me." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And somewhere above all of it, a room full of careful people deciding what I am. Again." Lira: "They're behind schedule. You already decided." Brom: "Years ago." Karis, writing: "Noted." Down the table, the man who used to file reports about him watched the road and said nothing, which — from him — was agreement.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:353:- [ ] **Book 4 → Book 5 seam verified against Book 4's architecture close:** Book 4 ends at semester's close — Cael sixteen, six confirmed fragments + Tide anomaly, assay enrollment renewed by the Ch21 evaluation, Lira formally Iron-tier Rank 1 (Ch24 Arbiter advancement), Brom's force-redirect flaw repaired (Ch19), Karis's tournament-clause note banked with one word ("Interesting."), Seln functionally switched and still posted, Vastin's file note filed (*Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.*), Withrow's tournament program declared, qualifying season "next year." Book 5 honors all of it: the ~three-month skip is explicit and staged in Chapter 1; the fielding clause is deployed as banked (Ch1–2) and its text matches Book 4 Ch24 verbatim (*participating academies shall field any practitioner appearing on their enrollment record*); Lira competes in the Iron bracket AS Iron R1; Brom's repaired defense is load-bearing in Ch11; the Vastin relationship builds strictly from the Ch22-B4 civil interview baseline — nothing warmer, nothing colder, until the Ch23 warning changes the terms honestly.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:355:- [ ] **SECRET discipline — the effect goes public, the mechanism does not:** The Book 3 SECRET (integration of witnessed abilities) remains known ONLY to the inner circle — Lira, Brom, Karis. What the Daeva match publishes is an inexplicable EVENT: a practitioner with no Storm record deploying Storm architecture mid-bout. No character outside the circle names, guesses, or approaches the mechanism; the public interpretations (Ch22) are enumerated and all wrong; the words "integration," "fragment," and "acquisition" never leave the log and the circle. **Seln does NOT learn the mechanism this book** — he witnesses the public event like everyone at Norhold; the asymmetry-debt (B4 Ch14) is re-surfaced in Ch19 (Shadow-adjacent held sealed to protect him, without his knowledge) and remains unpaid — available to the Book 6 era when he formally comes inside. Book 6's falsification SECRET and Book 8's Architect SECRET: untouched. Daeva learns only what she saw ("That was mine." / "A piece of it.") — an honest shape, no mechanism.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:356:- [ ] **Tournament staged per bible, exactly:** Three-year cycle and open registration honored (established B4 Ch2/Ch24); Halcenvane qualifies (Ch7); Cael participates via the fielding-clause technicality Karis found (Ch1–2), with the demonstration-provision enrollment as the record that puts him on the roster. Each companion fights separately BEFORE the companions fight together: Lira vs. speed specialist Zerin who outpaces her (Ch10 — loses the semifinal, extended her further than anyone at Norhold); Brom vs. Glass Path striker Marek who finds every gap in his defense (Ch11 — wins the continental Copper title by pricing his own gaps); Karis vs. information-advantage opponent Ivenne who knows her technique perfectly (Ch12 — wins by abandoning her documented self). Each is a full featured chapter with real arc weight, not summary. The together-fights follow: team trial semifinal loss to Auremont (Ch15, first Cael–Daeva contact, one exchange) and third-place trial win over Rhagen (Ch17). Halcenvane places THIRD (Ch17, ceremony Ch24).
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:358:- [ ] **Fragment count at book close:** 7 confirmed integrations — Wind-adjacent (B1, Lira), Pressure-adjacent (B1, Feryn), Iron-adjacent (B2 Ch13, Brom), Compression-adjacent (B2 Ch24, Reydan), Ember-adjacent (B3 Ch13, Karis), Shadow-adjacent (B4 Ch13, Seln), **Storm-adjacent (B5 Ch21, Daeva — first Gold-equivalent source, first stability-flagged notice, first acquisition witnessed publicly)**. Plus one anomaly entry (Tide-adjacent, B2 Ch19) — referenced ONLY in Ch14 and Ch24 inventories with the standing three words; NOT reproduced, NOT confirmed, NOT advanced, never counted as an eighth fragment. Trajectory check against bible: Book 6 close requires nine integrated abilities — 7 at B5 close leaves exactly two for Book 6's architecture to stage. Shadow-adjacent deployed NOWHERE public this book (Ch19 decision, Ch20–21 held) — the banking of the other five is formally spent (Ch19–20), a permanent change to his public record that Book 6 inherits.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:359:- [ ] **Companion status at book close:** Lira — Wind Path, Iron-tier Rank 1 formal; continental Iron semifinalist; Fenmark closed as a wound (Ch6); Silver-bracket ambition declared on-page (Ch10, Ch24) — the trajectory Book 6's walkout will cost her, deliberately built here. Brom — Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier formal; **continental Copper champion**; mandatory advancement review scheduled and PENDING at close (unresolved by design — Book 6 walkout stake); Velmere letter beat (sister's hand, one line) touched and banked, family thread still open. Karis — Ember Path, Iron-tier Rank 3; quarterfinal win over Ternhall's top rank by leaving her own documentation; both clause-technicalities (fielding, squad eligibility) hers. Seln — Shadow Path, Bronze-tier; full companion in function, Compact TA in form; sat at the table (Ch14) and traveled home with them (Ch24) — one step past B4's wall, no confession, no ceremony; null reports maintained; cache untouched; still posted at Halcenvane (Book 6 needs the desk to walk away from). Ephram — arc completed to open ally (Ch8, Ch15); remains at Halcenvane, available to Book 6.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:361:- [ ] **Naming — screened against the full B1/B2/B3/B4 collision registry:** New names this book: **Daeva** (Gold prodigy — bible-locked since the series bible's Book 5 entry and reserved in B4's checkpoint; she is the SANCTIONED sole exception to the no-new-D-names rule, which otherwise holds — no other new D-name appears), **Zerin** (Z initial, previously unused — screened vs. Seln/Sarel/Sella: distinct shape and sound), **Marek** (screened vs. Marrow, B1 bookmaker, last seen B1 — distinct role, era, and shape; noted), **Ivenne** (screened vs. Ilsev — I-v vs. I-l, distinct; vs. Yeni — distinct), **Umber** (screened vs. Ulric — distinct; chosen AFTER rejecting "Grale" for Gault-adjacency and "Loram" for Yorlan-adjacency, both same-role-space collisions), **Norhold** (host city — chosen AFTER rejecting "Calderis" for its collision with **Valdris**, the continent; no -mere suffix, screened vs. Naveth), **Auremont** (academy — screened vs. Ardenmere/Amrit: distinct), **Rhagen** (academy — screened vs. Reydan: RAY-dan vs. RAH-gen, person vs. institution, distinct; noted for drafting awareness), **Storm Path** (new Path name, consistent with the elemental register), **stormlane** (technique term), **the Silver Standard / the Concourse** (objects/places). Standing rules held: no new S-names near Seln, no new V-names near Vastin/Vell/Velmere, no new H-a/H-el names (Halcenvane remains that family's only recent member), no C-r family additions, no -essa names, no new -mere places, no bare "Iron Path" character anywhere near Brom (Marek is Glass; Zerin is Wind; "Iron Skin" spelled in full at every occurrence). Reserved names untouched: **Oryn (B7)**. The two Ch13 Silver volunteers are unnamed BY DESIGN (named-by-role, the old-yard-owner convention) to keep the collision surface small.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:363:- [ ] **LOCKED threads verified untouched or correctly maintained:** Tide-adjacent anomaly — inventory references only (Ch14, Ch24), three words, no Tide practitioner cast anywhere in the book (stated drafting constraint). [UNBOUND] — the word appears nowhere; one stratum-adjacent notebook beat (Ch2) with no term, no source, no advancement. Level 4 flag — advanced per trajectory (closed referral, private notes, unattributed citation in the convening), origin unreached, LOCKED until B6. Seln's cache — carried, unopened, contents unshown. Iron Skin watcher, market stranger, Hesk's history (letters only), Vell's session (letter reference at most), Coss's grade — all untouched. Records-broker client — advanced and substantially CLOSED as benign (Ch5, scouting economy) per B4's "hook, not a debt" framing; residue (the circulating dossier) logged as texture. NEW open threads created deliberately: Daeva's rematch promise (warm, Reydan-lineage); the unnamed faction above Vastin (cold, faceless, B6's engine); Brom's pending advancement review (B6 walkout stake).
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:364:- [ ] **The internal arc staged honestly at both poles:** "I can beat anyone at my tier" written in the log, in confidence, Chapter 4; cracked at midpoint against Silver, Chapter 13 ("I have never once stood on the ladder"); landed in full after the scoring crisis, Chapter 22 ("I am not at any tier" — arriving as truth, not wound). The tournament's structure carries the argument mechanically: Iron said no (brackets), Silver said no (exhibitions), Gold said no (Daeva), and the instrument said no in writing (the unscorable entry). The Chapter 24 closing holds the settled version without triumphalism — the book ends with the system, not Cael, unable to live with the answer.
