books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:13:*She meant it structurally,* the log noted. *Any other delegation's entry — deeper rosters, continental programs, champions in keeping. She'd keep ours: a rebuilt Wind champion, a mispriced wall, a researcher, a finished captain, and me. An academy three years into a program, betting its first continental appearance on the proposition that the paper is wrong about people. It's not sentiment. It's her actual institutional thesis, and Norhold is the examination.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:25:Lira said nothing at all, which Cael noted, and asked her about at the walls, and got the honest answer at once: "Fenmark used to tell us about Norhold. The ones who'd competed got to tell it at winter table. I stopped listening on purpose the year they threw me out." She watched the gate towers come up over the wagon's bow. "Turns out it's bigger than they said. Good. I'd have hated it if the liars had been accurate."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:45:The delegation quarter gave Halcenvane a floor of a guesting-house shared with two smaller academies — credentials at the tournament's registration hall, floor schedules, warm-up allocations, the whole administrative liturgy, which Seln processed for the delegation with a speed that left the neighboring academies' clerks visibly reassessing their careers — and the fame problem arrived within the hour, at continental scale, exactly as advertised.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:49:It arrived second as a knot of academy fighters from two delegations he hadn't met, who stopped their conversation when he crossed the courtyard and did not resume it until he had passed, and third as a Concourse steward who checked his credential against the printed card twice, not from doubt but from what was plainly the desire to have personally verified it, and fourth, and fifth, and by evening the delegation had adjusted its formation around him without a word — Brom drifting to his outside shoulder in crowds, Lira running interference on conversations, the old floor-craft of the found family redeployed to a new terrain.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:53:The printed sheets sold at four shops on the processional way and from two barrows in the Concourse's outer court, at a copper and a half the sheet, and there were — he bought the full set, methodically, to the visible delight of a barrow-keeper who recognized his customer halfway through the transaction and heroically said nothing — six distinct editions. THE ENROLLEE OF HALCENVANE in two versions, one heroic and one sinister, from the same printing house, which was its own lesson in the economy's honesty. THE UNCLASSIFIED, cruder, popular. A LIRA OF HALCENVANE sheet that rendered her taller and angrier than life and that he purchased in quantity for reasons of future diplomacy. A DAEVA OF AUREMONT edition of obvious best-selling status, in which the artist had spent all his skill on the storm and none on the face. And one sheet, newest, titled THE FIVE FROM THE BLUFF, which had the whole delegation as a group in woodcut approximation, Brom rendered as a mountain, and Seln — listed on every roster as staff, a clerk's line nobody's dossier had ever bothered to read — placed by the artist not at the margin where the rosters put him but inside the group, at the table's own edge, a lean figure with a ledger, one of them.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:55:He laid the set out on the guesting-house table that night and the delegation examined its own mythology with the range of responses the year had made predictable: Lira demanded to know why the artist had given her a scowl ("That's not my scowl. My scowl is better organized"), Brom studied the mountain version of himself in silence and folded his copy into the kit where the Velmere letters lived, Karis annotated her own absence from three of the six editions with genuine professional interest ("the economy prices visibility, and I read as staff — good"), and Seln looked at the lean figure with the ledger for a long moment and said, in his flattest administrative register, "Inaccurate. The office's ledger is larger," which was, the table eventually decided, a joke, the season's first confirmed sighting of one.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:57:*The city has printed us,* the log recorded. *Versions of versions, a copper and a half. Note what the sheet-artists understood that the dossier-compilers didn't: where Seln stands. The rosters file him under staff and the dossiers never look past the filing. A woodcutter with a barrow watched the group walk past once and put him inside it. Instruments measure what they're calibrated for. Artists just look.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:99:Lira's Iron pool resolved first. A manageable opening line, a second round that would want scouting — and then, on the semifinal line, where the pool's geometry had always been going to put her: ZERIN, AUREMONT. She read it without surprise and with total attention, the way she read scouting sheets, and said, "Good. No early ambush. Time to be ready," and stayed at the board one moment more, measuring the distance between her name and that one, which was three printed lines and six years of doors.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:103:Ephram's pool read hard and fair — a first-round bout against the coastal column's second entry, a probable round-of-sixteen meeting with the lake academy's methodical Shield, and a pool line that kept him clear of both Lira and Karis until a hypothetical final. He read it twice, nodded once, and produced his verdict in the cohort's economic register: "Nothing free, nothing unfair. Correct tournament." Then, because the new Ephram carried the old one's honesty upgraded rather than replaced: "Round of sixteen is my price of admission. Everything past it is profit, and I intend to trade."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:19:The second recognition was at the validation table, and it ran the other direction. He walked the delegation's credential file over with Seln — assessment-office business, entirely ordinary — and the senior of the two Compact officers checked the five competitor entries against the registry list at checking speed until the fifth, where the registry list gave her nothing to check against, and she looked up, and Cael watched a professional decide in real time which procedure applied to a person who wasn't in the book.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:21:"Enrollment basis," she said finally, to Seln rather than to him, holding out a hand. Seln produced the certified extract — prepared, tabbed, already in the folder's front pocket, because of course it was. She read it whole. Then she initialed the block, wrote a word in her own margin that Cael read upside down — *basis sighted* — and said, to Cael directly, with a registry officer's whole compressed carefulness: "You'll show this at every meet. They won't get faster about it."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:31:Lira's Iron-bracket debut was the first bout of the second morning — the card's compilers, whether by chance or a steward's sense of theater, had given the certified reclamation the day's opening slot, and the gallery had filled early for it, the wool town's whole appetite for the season's stories arriving with its breakfast still in hand — and she walked to it differently than she had ever walked to a circuit match, and Cael, watching from the gallery rail with Brom, could not name the difference until Brom named it for him.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:35:The opponent was an Iron Rank 2 from the river academy two towns north, Stone Path, a broad patient young man whose profile Rooke's margin had rendered as *honest, readable*. He was both.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:39:Lira let him have the first exchange almost entirely. She ran it at circuit tempo, orbiting the rise's downhill margin, offering entries and withdrawing them, and Cael, scoring from the rail, watched her buy the three facts she'd come for at the price of two flags against: the tremor-read ran through his front foot, not both; his counters launched a full beat after the read, not a half; and he defended the slope's downhill edge a handspan lazier than its crown, because nobody attacks uphill.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:41:Second exchange, she attacked uphill. One burst, spent flat out — not at him but past his left shoulder along the rise's edge, through the handspan he was lazy about — and then she was above him on his own slope with his stance's whole geometry inverted, and reading Wind and answering Wind were different competences at different prices, and he had budgeted for neither at that angle. The exchange ended with the flat crack of a landing given up and the judges' flags together, and the gallery made the sound galleries make when the shape of a bout declares itself early.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:53:"I know," she said, without any flourish at all, which was the certification talking, and then, because it was Lira: "Today. Against him. The sheet says the eastern academies sent a Wind entry Rooke can't price. *That's* the season."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:61:The buried one gave Brom a real morning. He was fast, seasoned, and wrong-footed exactly nobody with his tier — the gallery's bookmaker board had him at odds that said the chalk knew — and he went at Brom's guard for two exchanges with a fair-day fighter's whole inventory. Brom absorbed it the way Brom absorbed everything, unhurried, cataloguing, giving up the small scores that cost nothing and conceding none that compounded; and in the third exchange the pricing came due, and he closed the door. Iron Skin amplification, pre-loaded at the hinge of an entry the man had used nine times because it worked nine times; the redirect put him on the boards, and the flags went up, and Brom helped him rise with the particular courtesy he kept for people the system had filed wrong.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:83:The delegation's evenings at the wool town assembled their own small institution — the inn's corner table annexed by the same silent mechanics as every table before it, the day's charts passed left to right, Rooke's six-sentence reviews delivered over the stew — and Cael catalogued the assembling with a year-old satisfaction: the found family's field configuration, deployed for the first time with credentials in every pocket. Ephram went through the Iron bracket's first day like a man running downhill, and the gallery began, by the second morning, to do the thing that Cael had felt from the inside for years and had never before watched happen to somebody else: it began to watch him.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:85:Not for rank. His rank was ordinary — Iron Rank 4, solid third-year paper. What drew the rail was the way the work looked: Rooke's cohort doctrine, run clean at meet tempo by its best current product, every entry a half-beat earlier than the regional eye expected, every recovery already resolved into the next position. He won three bouts in five exchanges total. A knot of students from two rival academies took up station at his floor and stayed there, and a girl with a notebook — every academy had one; Cael checked the reflex and found it fond — charted him bout by bout.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:101:The first was an Iron Rank 6, a quarry-guild journeyman ten years his senior who had read a version of the dossier and wanted, he said at the line with disarming plainness, "to see it myself before the story finishes cooking." The second was a Bronze Rank 3 from the host town, a meet steward's own cousin, who wanted what men who volunteer against strangers always wanted, and had the look of someone who had been the best in his valley long enough to mistake it for a fact about the world.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:107:Cael declined the invitation four times in the first exchange, which was the exchange's entire content — pressure offered, angle refused, reset — and the crowd, wanting spectacle, began to get restless, and the quarryman, who was honest, began to grin, because he could read what the crowd couldn't: the refusals were costing him more than they cost the boy. Every herding run he built, Cael was gone from at the second step — not fleeing it, *pre-empting* it, the Iron-adjacent surface read working the man's weight-shifts a half-beat ahead of the movement, though all the panel would ever see was a young fighter with peculiar timing — and a leverage fight that never reaches its leverage is a man pushing a door that opens away from him.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:109:Second and third exchange, Cael spent his documented Wind-adjacent evasion at its documented working rate, no repetition past the ceiling, and turned the geometry around: now the wall was the quarryman's problem, arrived at not by being driven but by increments of refused angle, each reset a half-step nearer the stone than the last, so gradual the man never felt himself herded. In the fourth, with the wall a stride behind the quarryman's heels and every line of retreat priced, Cael took the exchange at contact — one entry, clean, scored — and stepped back before the leverage game could close on him even once.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:115:*He caught it,* the log recorded. *The panel scored a conservative Iron-band win. The one man standing inside the bout felt the management. Note for the season: the Standard is not the only instrument in a hall. Every opponent is an instrument, and the good ones read back.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:129:The meet closed the second evening with standings read from the guild hall steps: Halcenvane second of six delegations on accumulated points, behind the river academy's deeper Iron entry and ahead of everything else, which for a first meet in eighteen years was a result Withrow's office would file without comment and remember forever.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:131:Rooke took the standings sheet without expression, folded it into the coaching file, and gave the delegation its review on the guild hall steps in the season's now-standard six sentences: "Second of six. Lira, your morning-after warm-up will tell me what four bursts cost — don't dress it. Brom, nothing; continue. Karis, your second bout took an exchange longer than your first for a weaker opponent, and I know it's because you were watching the panel instead of the man, and I'm allowing it, and now you know I'm allowing it. Ephram: finished is further than good, keep going. Cael — " and here the coach paused, looking at the file, at his own exhibition notes clipped inside it, professional puzzlement worn openly — "you fought exactly as well as you intended to, which is a sentence I've never had to say to a fighter in thirty years, and I haven't decided what my job is inside it yet. Wagons at first light."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-03.md:133:The wagons loaded in the blue dark. Cael took the last bench, as he took it every time, where the tailboard gave him the road behind — old habit, three years deep, the watcher's seat — and found the habit obsolete: the road behind held only meet traffic, carts and lanterns and two academies' wagons in loose file, all of it listed, all of it printed in a program. Rotation men did not follow what a schedule already delivered. The town's lights went small behind them the way all towns' lights went small, one road at a time, and Lira slept against the sideboard with her kit for a pillow, and the river ran the wagons north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:9:- Read before drafting: source `chapter-03.md` and `chapter-04.md`. The previous ending is this edition's `manuscript/chapter-06.md`. From `BOOK_MAP.md`, read §2 items 13–17, §3 A (wool-town and mill-town rows) and B 1–3, §5 (Seln), §7, §8 (quarryman, yard-master), §10 items 7–8, 20–21, 35–36, and §12 C3, C8, C10. Also `STATE_LEDGER.md` (latest section).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:13:The wagons roll south two days to the wool town, five weeks after convocation. The card says *scheduled per demonstration-exhibition provisions*. Lira's first bracket bout as a formal Iron is the next morning's opening slot. Cael rides the tailboard out of a habit that the printed program has made obsolete.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:21:- **Lira's debut** against the Stone Path on the sheep-market slope: three exchanges. "Nobody gets to be surprised by me anymore. I have to actually be this good." Log the bill of four bursts, one over rate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:26:- **The protest ruling** (verbatim) at the waystation. Karis: "The category is going to hold you exactly as long as nobody makes you." Lira's two days of half work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:28:- **Bracket days, on the page:** Lira vs the guild champion (the pillar corner banked two days; "Your floor has furniture"); **Karis vs Lira (~1,800)**, "a three-exchange family argument" in which Karis loses and collects data; **Ephram's first regional final (~1,600)**.
--- exact protected/withheld probes in M4 ---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:"No," Rooke agreed. "You didn't. Listen, because I'll say it once and then we'll both get on with the work." He did not lower his voice; he never needed to. "A tired man doesn't do what he's decided. Deciding takes breath, and he has none spare. He does the thing he practised the longest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:*He does the thing he practised the longest.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:THE CORE MUST BE FREE BEFORE DECLARATION. CHECK THE PIN.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:*Hall three (east), north bay, sixth bell, Third-day. Apparatus failure during a supervised floor hour: the retaining pin of a resistance post sheared under load at the seventh setting, locking the core during a Force Path third-year's fourth declaration of his third set and returning the delivery off the pad. Subject was at the bay's edge on an adjoining footwork drill, signed for by the Ash Path instructor, and intervened. The third-year was not struck. No injury requiring the infirmary. The academy's floor warden attended within the quarter-hour, withdrew all four posts on the bluff from use, and has recommended inspection and replacement of every retaining pin in the academy's apparatus; her draft and the wing's log are enclosed. Response consistent with documented baseline.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:83:He filed the report. Then he sat for a while with the file drawer open, not filing anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:133:*Enrollee (assay provision) present at bay edge; moved to intervene; received the release on a guard arm; no injury reported. Third-year not struck.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:163:"The conditions are met. If you directed it, I believe it would complete." Her hands closed the notebook on its single paragraph and stayed there, flat on the grey cover. "Which is why we have to talk about whether you may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:63:"That's not a demonstration you're stealing. That's a weapon you're learning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:*Last year's me was afraid of not-knowing what I do. This year's version is worse: knowing exactly, and deciding anyway. Write it down before, not after. That's the whole difference between me and the system.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:23:*Karis on the stair afterward, when the others had gone up: "An instrument doesn't change sides. It changes readings."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:143:Fiske spoke first. "You gave me a false first exchange."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:147:"I gave you a true one. You just assumed it was all of them."
