155:"There's a form," said Fiske. "For an advancement evaluation. The registry sends an Arbiter if the form goes up. It wants one signature that isn't mine." She turned the cup in her hands. "Two seasons I've sat on top of that sheet, with Rank Eight on my certificate. Two crowns. Every bout on this hill worth having. The porter polishes the stud beside my name on Fourth-days. And in all that time nobody has picked up the pen. Not because anyone decided not to. Because nobody ever had a reason to. I was winning. Why would anybody send the winner away?"
267:A challenge upon the holder's line, it said, in Bracken's small upright hand, was fought not on the ladder's terms but on the final's: four exchanges, each on the table's glass, each to the cleaner touch or called even, and the bout to whoever held more touches at the end of the fourth. A drawn bout left the holder on the top line. The top line, at the season's close, fought its semifinal on the earlier of the two semifinal sessions, and so came to the final with a session's more rest than the second line.
179:The rig was where he kept it, rolled in a meal sack behind the copper. Three canvas bags of sand, stitched along every seam with the waxed thread Brom used on his boots, each with a loop of rope at the neck, marked in charcoal *light*, *middle*, *heavy*. A length of cord knotted at measured lengths. A wooden peg he could wedge into the crack between two flags to mark exactly where his heels belonged. Brom had sewn the bags in an evening without asking what they were for, and had asked only, when he handed them over, whether Cael wanted a fourth for luck.
